# Configuration

## `KarboneConfig`

`KarboneConfig` is the immutable, per-client configuration behind every `Karbone` instance:

| Field             | Default                        | Meaning                                                                    |
|-------------------|---------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `baseUrl`         | —                                | `https://api.carbone.io` or an on-premise URL such as `http://localhost:4000`. Trailing slash optional. |
| `token`           | `null`                          | Bearer token. `null` sends no `Authorization` header.                       |
| `apiVersion`      | `null`                          | `carbone-version` header. `null` omits it (what on-premise expects); cloud uses `KarboneConfig.LATEST_API_VERSION` (5). |
| `connectTimeout`  | 10 seconds                      | TCP connect timeout.                                                         |
| `requestTimeout`  | 60 seconds                      | Per-request timeout. Carbone's own synchronous render timeout is 60 s.       |
| `retry`           | `RetryPolicy.None`              | Retries transport failures only — API errors are never retried.             |
| `json`            | `KarboneConfig.DefaultJson`      | `kotlinx.serialization.json.Json` used for request bodies and response parsing. |
| `userAgent`       | `"karbone/<version>"`            | `User-Agent` header sent with every request.                                |

## Building a client

`Karbone.cloud` and `Karbone.onPremise` both go through `KarboneConfig.Builder`, exposed as a trailing lambda:

```kotlin
val cloud = Karbone.cloud("YOUR_API_KEY") {
  requestTimeout = 30.seconds
  retry = RetryPolicy.OnTransportError(maxAttempts = 3, backoff = 300.milliseconds)
}

val onPremise = Karbone.onPremise("http://localhost:4000", token = "YOUR_JWT") {
  connectTimeout = 5.seconds
}
```

Defaults differ deliberately between the two factories:

- `Karbone.cloud(apiKey)` sets `baseUrl` to `KarboneConfig.CLOUD_BASE_URL` and `apiVersion` to
  `KarboneConfig.LATEST_API_VERSION`, so every request carries `carbone-version: 5`.
- `Karbone.onPremise(baseUrl, token)` leaves `apiVersion` at `null`: no `carbone-version` header is sent, matching what
  self-hosted `carbone-ee` expects (see [On-premise](on-premise.qd)). `token` itself is optional, since on-premise
  authentication is disabled by default.

Retries (`RetryPolicy.OnTransportError`) only cover connection resets, timeouts and other I/O failures — never a Carbone API
error such as a 4xx or 5xx response, which is always surfaced as the corresponding `KarboneError` on the first attempt.

## Per-call headers

`KarboneConfig` configures headers for the whole client; a single render can add its own through `RenderOptions.headers` (or
the builder's `header(name, value)`), for example to relay a token to a downstream service via
`carbone-egress-header-authorization`, without changing the client's own `Authorization`:

```kotlin
karbone.renders.render(template, data) {
  header("carbone-egress-header-authorization", "Bearer downstream-token")
}
```

## Reading a client's settings

`Karbone` does not expose its `KarboneConfig`: the token stays private and the configuration is a construction detail. Two read-only
values are available because they are useful in startup logs and health checks:

```kotlin
logger.info { "Carbone at ${karbone.baseUrl} (api version ${karbone.apiVersion ?: "server default"})" }
```

## Logging

Karbone logs through kotlin-logging under the `com.ekino.oss.karbone` logger names and stays silent at `INFO` and above in the
normal path. See the dedicated [Logging](logging.qd) page for backends, levels, what is never logged and configuration examples.

